        {
          "id": "over-fuelling-is-the-root-cause",
          "title": "Over-fuelling is the Root Cause",
          "description": "Black smoke means your engine is burning too much fuel — over-fuelling. This is most common on diesel engines.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "MAF sensor failure (£100) Wrong air readings cause the ECU to inject excess fuel.",
                "Injector issues (£120-£900 each) Leaking, sticking, or dripping injector nozzles.",
                "Turbo failure (£450-£3,000+) Insufficient airflow delivery for combustion demand.",
                "EGR valve blocked (£300-£600) Recirculation blockage restricting fresh air intake.",
                "Air filter clogged (£20-£50) Severe air intake restriction (simple initial fix)."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "📊 In our 24,650 quote dataset, 70% of black smoke cases were caused by sensor or injector issues — not catastrophic mechanical engine failure."
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "level": "h4",
              "id": "",
              "text": "Safe to drive?"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Black smoke alone is not an immediate stop-driving emergency. However, continued driving will:"
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Damage your DPF (£1,000–£3,500 replacement)",
                "Reduce fuel economy significantly",
                "Cause an immediate MOT failure"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "What people think vs reality"
            }
          ],
          "links": [],
          "source": {
            "sectionId": "over-fuelling-is-the-root-cause",
            "order": 1
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "when-black-smoke-needs-urgent-attention",
          "title": "⚠️ WHEN BLACK SMOKE NEEDS URGENT ATTENTION",
          "description": "Black smoke is rarely an immediate stop-driving emergency - but these symptoms require prompt action:",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Smoke is constant (even at idle)",
                "Engine warning light is on",
                "Loss of power or rough running",
                "DPF warning light appears (diesel)"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "MOT Implications: Any visible black smoke from the exhaust is an automatic MOT failure. Don't wait until your MOT to fix it."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Book Diagnostic"
